# Cloudflare Pages cache-control config.
#
# THE CONDITION THIS FILE SET FOR ITSELF HAS BEEN MET.
#
# It used to say: "Move to long-lived immutable caching only once/if the
# build gains content-hashed filenames." scripts/build.js now gains
# them — every stylesheet and script it emits carries `?v=` and eight
# hex of the SHA-256 of its own bytes.
#
# WHY THAT MATTERED MORE THAN SPEED. The conservative setting was not
# merely slow, it was wrong in a way nobody had measured. On 19 August
# 2026 a deploy landed and the live domain went on serving the previous
# css/brand.css for forty-eight minutes — cf-cache-status HIT, age
# 2868, against an edge max-age of four hours. The HTML is
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` and updates at once. So for up to four
# hours after every deploy a returning visitor could receive NEW MARKUP
# AGAINST OLD CSS, which is not a slow update but a broken page, and it
# is invisible to everybody who happens to hard-refresh.
#
# A fingerprinted URL cannot be stale by construction: a file that
# changed has a new URL, and a file that did not keeps the old one and
# stays cached. So the year is now safe AND correct, where an hour was
# neither.
#
# The paths here are unhashed on purpose — the fingerprint is a query
# string, so /css/brand.css still matches this rule with or without it.

/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate

/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/sitemap.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY HEADERS
#
# Added after an audit found the site sending none at all. Each of the
# headers below is safe to apply site-wide — none of them can break a
# page that currently works.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, AND WHY:
#
#   Content-Security-Policy. A script-src policy is the header worth
#   having and it is the one that can silently break sign-in. Clerk
#   loads its SDK from a Frontend API host derived at runtime from the
#   publishable key (js/clerk-loader.js), so the correct allowlist
#   depends on which Clerk instance is configured, and Google Fonts adds
#   two more origins. A wrong CSP does not fail a build or a test — it
#   fails one real student trying to sign in, silently, in production.
#
#   It is not omitted because it does not matter. It is omitted because
#   shipping it honestly requires one thing this environment cannot do:
#   loading the deployed site and completing a real sign-in with the CSP
#   active. That is a ten-minute check against the live domain, and it
#   should be done before a CSP is added, not after.
#
# Permissions-Policy grants microphone to this origin only, because the
# Listening Lab records the learner — removing it would break the single
# most distinctive part of the programme. Camera and geolocation are
# denied outright: nothing here uses either, and a permission nothing
# uses should not be available to ask for.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: microphone=(self), camera=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
